fD3gK7Ui%2FVOll0WZQ8ENcy0BZsTum%2B5jpCR%2F3K75HTUmOYC&X-Amz-Signature=c1201cf8f3bb31ead17df0ec2fbc748f86a89db0d8d12de145f263b3c6307b38&X-Amz-SignedHeaders=host&x-amz-checksum-mode=ENABLED&x-id=GetObject)\ndating app where men can’t post photos, only use their words to attract women \n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nSell a Bubble MVP SaaS template\n- Launch your SaaS in 1 week \n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n[https://hireflix.com/en?utm_source=google&utm_medium=cpc&utm_id=17501857862&utm_campaign=GGL\\|SEM\\|EN\\|BRD\\|All&utm_term=hireflix&utm_content=604036878441&gad_source=1&gclid=CjwKCAiAhP67BhAVEiwA2E_9gyrLKo5BRWX2r1YSSlh_vGeSO2Q8B0F8z5PnDuon1gEFnSmZikTi4RoCaTkQAvD_BwE]({{https://hireflix.com/en?utm_source=google&utm_medium=cpc&utm_id=17501857862&utm_campaign=GGL%7CSEM%7CEN%7CBRD%7CAll&utm_term=hireflix&utm_content=604036878441&gad_source=1&gclid=CjwKCAiAhP67BhAVEiwA2E_9gyrLKo5BRWX2r1YSSlh_vGeSO2Q8B0F8z5PnDuon1gEFnSmZikTi4RoCaTkQAvD_BwE}})\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nLook into N8N\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<page url=\"{{https://www.notion.so/cf0fd0b8bc0b4611b8a7ddd34d5ae850}}\">Idea Dump</page>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\ngetting early users:<br>Ticket turnaround 48hrs\n- on call issue\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nwhat does every app need: \n- landing page \n- sign up/login \n- dashboard \n- payment gateway/billing flow/pricing page \n- Personal information \n- Terms and conditions/privacy policy \n- Support/feedback/contact us \n- chat \n- admin dashboard \n- AI \n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nbubble template \n<empty-block/>\n![](https://prod-files-secure.s3.us-west-2.amazonaws.com/f517d51c-d559-4a6e-a307-29e438b5b332/08105e88-dff5-4af9-92ac-0e197e6b045d/Screen_Shot_2025-01-17_at_2.32.00_pm.png?X-Amz-Algorithm=AWS4-HMAC-SHA256&X-Amz-Content-Sha256=UNSIGNED-PAYLOAD&X-Amz-Credential=ASIAZI2LB4666F2FQNX3%2F20260223%2Fus-west-2%2Fs3%2Faws4_request&X-Amz-Date=20260223T085030Z&X-Amz-Expires=3600&X-Amz-Security-Token=IQoJb3JpZ2luX2VjEBEaCXVzLXdlc3QtMiJHMEUCIQDmrL7OdVI5NkcULlyjWeeyjikuyVOda2YTuwSRy5IMoQIgWS7qpc%2FWhB%2BFh%2BSQzazQUkJLOogXyBqmE4%2Fc0%2FdPhs0qiAQI2v%2F%2F%2F%2F%2F%2F%2F%2F%2F%2FARAAGgw2Mzc0MjMxODM4MDUiDEwu1G6vdJul9XFo6SrcA%2FXQnslwFwZevobODrtY7h%2B0CoLXOAxyLQSB%2FPtcTjfwBl%2B%2BFPQOc1VcY9uHAE6u%2FFEB65eebo9j1cL9k1DPW9cyGVut%2Bd4qYoyu2GNoIeVi57U9uFrUcyObhgWmsFBy%2Fnvqq0uTRyYg0fObAHsk4NoMjXcVPfyhCtjREWWxnkNOdr51Om%2FTHY0czNRNbNZOU3DWsg6tSd04poGdQiLiGVQyqpSBfJqjiZHNGO0Wvkq27LBP22LVu19c1TfVhUERpqU2wRv%2F47B2guMEK3xYbcfO67POCQPiwD4bP7T90YVYwk9xbIsWc5A%2F%2BB5uOzb5uxcFoEReFC2438QBjrMuRXMFTKzwz1c6CaqLHv3zSS2%2B8dgI13io1dnOdnC2LPDblZLpe78J5VWG4hDw3t7eFEqlAcQmqGNdyyHpUGtvIv7bV4EFjZ0ERA3wDOvS9zpKeYacPPTaBiTE8Uv4dhlLIyDTnUCS5zUligh808J4UNR%2Bpg%2F6LqXfDkAK2lK5CGDrXUi2OZz5j5O1%2F0Ae7PvGHOsytFnnw8tkUl4ojOTn%2BUti8npBo5f%2F5aj8Zu17%2BEyPJCoayh2Owa4jR7bZlz6rA9RGrbABj8PASWXkfyfFlXjOpLZrYL3k1BPWj3i7MJil8MwGOqUBJ%2F9%2BqJH9oTN0SMYCLdcCXbP70PNK6AvSpfPP7KIfxp%2BXhBEzi28SbYQ4ltcJUZH8qSGJP%2FSkn9TeNPHtptSsofii%2B2x1NcaJHBUniokErJ4YGMh5cZpdcJQN%2FFp6sUR7ebwwQ%2BFOnreyKqHBlW650qjmK81WiqrtL0P2dNlOwm25qfD3gK7Ui%2FVOll0WZQ8ENcy0BZsTum%2B5jpCR%2F3K75HTUmOYC&X-Amz-Signature=db4a58737fb451282031b35d2808944d78b8be6278beaca95b1dfaa1f34b7540&X-Amz-SignedHeaders=host&x-amz-checksum-mode=ENABLED&x-id=GetObject)\n<empty-block/>\nLinkedin\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nSellable skills: \nBubble dev \nWireframing/figma design \nLanding pages \nSaaS/founder consulting \nMarketing ( SaaS marketing) \nVideo editing \nContent idea generation \n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<br>[https://docs.openbuild.io/introduction/the-library]({{https://docs.openbuild.io/introduction/the-library}})\n<empty-block/>\napi docs:[https://www.altexsoft.com/blog/api-documentation/]({{https://www.altexsoft.com/blog/api-documentation/}})\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nFreelance sites:\n- Upwork \n- Contra \n- Fiver\n- freelancer\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n**READ Sprint by Jake Knapp**\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n**The power of customer misbehavior:**\nAllow customers to use your product to solve problems that were not intended to be solved with your product.\nSome of the buggest innovations come from monitoring what customers wanted to do outside of the what we had designed and the product’s original intentions. Learn what problem they are trying to solve and why they believe your product might provide the right foundation. Build on that.\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nharrytheindefatigable\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n[https://flowsavvy.app/pricing]({{https://flowsavvy.app/pricing}})\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nCall with Raffi Questions:\n<empty-block/>\nFor your sales call, the goal is to understand the client’s needs, budget, timeline, and expectations while also positioning yourself as the best fit for the job. Here are some key questions to ask:\n---\n### **1. Understanding the Business & Problem**\n- **What does your business do, and how does this app fit into your broader goals?**\n- **What problem are you trying to solve with this app?**\n- **What are the key pain points you’re experiencing right now?**\n- **Have you tried any existing solutions or alternatives? If so, what didn’t work?**\n---\n### **2. App Scope & Features**\n- **What are the core features you absolutely need for the app?**\n- **Are there any ‘nice-to-have’ features that aren’t essential but would be beneficial?**\n- **Do you have a specific platform in mind (iOS, Android, Web, or all three)?**\n- **Do you have any technical constraints or existing systems the app needs to integrate with?**\n- **How many users do you expect the app to have at launch and in the long term?**\n---\n### **3. Budget & Timeline**\n- **Do you have a budget range in mind for this project?**\n- **What’s your ideal launch date? Are there any hard deadlines we need to consider?**\n- **Are you looking for an MVP (Minimum Viable Product) first, or a fully-featured app from the start?**\n---\n### **4. Design & User Experience**\n- **Do you have a specific design or branding vision for the app?**\n- **Are there any apps or platforms you admire that you’d like us to take inspiration from?**\n- **Do you have existing wireframes, mockups, or a product spec, or do you need help defining that?**\n---\n### **5. Post-Launch & Maintenance**\n- **How do you plan to maintain and update the app after launch?**\n- **Would you need ongoing support, hosting, or updates from us?**\n- **Do you have a marketing or user acquisition plan for the app?**\n---\n### **6. Decision-Making & Next Steps **\n- **Are there other stakeholders involved in the decision-making process?**\n- **What does your decision-making process look like for choosing a development partner?**\n- **What’s the next step after this call? Would you like us to prepare a proposal or estimate?**\n---\nThese questions should give you a solid understanding of the project while also allowing you to demonstrate your expertise and value. Let me know if you want to refine them further!\n<empty-block/>\nRoastmyUI night  \n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nHypothesis:  Big established no-code companies will require labourers in the future \n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<page url=\"{{https://www.notion.so/12f348f436348053a9e2f4aafee2ae93}}\">SaaS</page>\n<page url=\"{{https://www.notion.so/118348f4363480c2850bf1bfb738ad27}}\">Codeless Society (Course) </page>\n<page url=\"{{https://www.notion.so/9af28eeec2934258a4386cf9d5c7a29f}}\">Being in Product </page>\n<page url=\"{{https://www.notion.so/122348f4363480b4be72d170aa8899b5}}\">SaaS Litmus Test </page>\n<page url=\"{{https://www.notion.so/112348f43634808fb297e8574c80bc82}}\">Schedule </page>\n<page url=\"{{https://www.notion.so/bef16558fdaa4e0688f9f4c3a8afd27d}}\">Product Team </page>\n<page url=\"{{https://www.notion.so/13b348f43634807984cdc76c2ca1472d}}\">Web or mobile? How to decide:</page>\n<page url=\"{{https://www.notion.so/e1f8d45da1344b4eb61ff185a673e1ea}}\">About The Codeless Founder </page>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nUpdate Github from Cursor \nPull request in Github \nRedeploy github changes in Vercel\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nSales for Mike \n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\ncool marketing company: \nInstant.one\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nCurrent Projects: \n1. Discopay\n2. Watchcrow\n3. Suds\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nI built this in cursor - if you’re interested in doing this, follow my channel below: \nDave McManus Accerator \nYour idea to market in 8 weeks \nCursor \nSupabase \nCursor and \nCheck buildcamp \n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nCodeExportAgency \n- Replicate bubble app into code \n- Include a 1 hour tutorial including \n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nSoftware I have built:\n- Inventory management system (app)\n- Watch marketplace (app)\n- Automated lead generation (Workflow)\n- Payment service with gambling feature (app)\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n \n<empty-block/>\nDifferent tech stacks:\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nCore Technologies:\n- **React** (v18): The main UI library for building the app.\n- **TypeScript**: The codebase is written in TypeScript for type safety.\n- **Vite**: Used as the build tool and development server for fast refresh and optimized builds.\n<empty-block/>\n**UI & Component Libraries**\n- **Radix UI**: Extensive use of Radix UI primitives (@radix-ui/react-\\*) for accessible, unstyled UI components.\n- **shadcn/ui**: The presence of many custom UI components in src/components/ui and the use of design tokens and patterns suggest the use of the shadcn/ui system, which builds on top of Radix UI.\n- **Tailwind CSS**: Utility-first CSS framework for styling, with plugins like @tailwindcss/typography and tailwindcss-animate for enhanced typography and animations.\n- **Lucide React**: For iconography.\n- **Sonner**: For toast notifications.\n- **Embla Carousel**: For carousel/slider components.\n- **Recharts**: For data visualization and charts.\n<empty-block/>\n### **Frontend Framework**\n- **Next.js** (\\^15.3.0)\n- Modern React-based framework for SSR, SSG, and client-side rendering.\n- Uses the /app directory structure (App Router).\n- Bootstrapped with create-next-app.\n### **2. Programming Language**\n- **JavaScript** (ES6+)\n- **TypeScript** types are present in dev dependencies, but the main code appears to be JavaScript.\n### **3. UI & Styling**\n- **React** (\\^19.0.0)\n- **React DOM** (\\^19.0.0)\n- **Tailwind CSS** (\\^3.4.1)\n- Utility-first CSS framework for styling.\n- **PostCSS** (\\^8)\n- **Framer Motion** (\\^12.16.0)\n- For animations and transitions.\n- **Lucide React** (\\^0.488.0)\n- Icon library.\n- **Lottie** (@lottiefiles/dotlottie-react, @lottiefiles/react-lottie-player)\n- For animated vector graphics.\n- **@splinetool/react-spline**\n- For 3D/interactive graphics.\n### **4. State Management**\n- No dedicated state management library (like Redux or Zustand) is present; state is managed via React hooks.\n### **5. APIs & Data Fetching**\n- **Axios** (\\^1.7.9)\n- For HTTP requests.\n- **Supabase** (@supabase/supabase-js \\^2.49.1)\n- Used for authentication, database, and storage.\n- **Stripe** (@stripe/react-stripe-js, @stripe/stripe-js)\n- For payment processing.\n### **6. Authentication**\n- **Supabase Auth**\n- Email/password and OAuth flows.\n- User roles and metadata handled via Supabase.\n- **Custom Auth Flows**\n- Components like AuthModal and AuthFlow for handling sign-up, sign-in, and merchant onboarding.\n### **7. Database & Backend**\n- **Supabase Database**\n- Managed Postgres database via Supabase.\n- Supabase Storage for file uploads (e.g., merchant logos).\n- **API Routes**\n- Custom API endpoints under /app/api/ for business logic.\n### **8. Payments**\n- **Stripe**\n- Integration for handling payments.\n### **9. Other Libraries**\n- **canvas-confetti**\n- For confetti effects.\n- **qrcode.react**\n- For generating QR codes.\n- **uuid**\n- For generating unique IDs.\n- **react-error-boundary**\n- For error handling in React components.\n### **10. Development & Tooling**\n- **ESLint** (\\^9)\n- Linting and code quality.\n- **@types/node**, **@types/react**\n- TypeScript type definitions for Node and React.\n- **Shopify CLI & App** (in devDependencies)\n- Present, but not clearly used in the main app logic.\n### **11. Deployment**\n- **Vercel** (recommended in README)\n- Next.js is optimized for Vercel deployment.\n- **Environment Variables**\n- Used for Supabase keys and URLs.\n### **12. Other Notes**\n- **Responsive & Accessible UI**\n- Tailwind and custom components are used for responsive layouts and accessibility.\n- **Design Consistency**\n- Custom design tokens and consistent component structure, as per your user rules.\n---\n**Summary Table:**\n<table>\n<tr>\n<td>Layer</td>\n<td>Technology/Libraries Used</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Frontend</td>\n<td>Next.js, React, Tailwind CSS, Framer Motion, Lucide, Lottie, Spline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>State</td>\n<td>React hooks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>API/Data</td>\n<td>Axios, Supabase JS, Stripe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Auth</td>\n<td>Supabase Auth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Backend/DB</td>\n<td>Supabase (Postgres, Storage)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Payments</td>\n<td>Stripe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tooling</td>\n<td>ESLint, PostCSS, TypeScript types, Shopify CLI (dev only)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deployment</td>\n<td>Vercel (suggested), Environment Variables</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Misc</td>\n<td>canvas-confetti, qrcode.react, uuid, react-error-boundary</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n---\nIf you need a more granular breakdown (e.g., which files use which libraries, or a dependency graph), let me know!\napp\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nProject switch checklist:\n### **Project Switch Checklist**\n### 🧭 Terminal Context\n- [ ] `cd` into the correct project folder\n- [ ] Confirm the terminal is not still pointing at the previous repo\n- [ ] (Optional) Run `git status` to verify you’re on the right branch and project\n### 🌐 Dev Server\n- [ ] Kill any running dev server from the previous project (`Ctrl+C`)\n- [ ] Choose a unique port if needed (`-port 3001`, `PORT=3001 npm run dev`)\n- [ ] Run `npm install` if it’s your first time in the repo\n### 📦 Environment Variables\n- [ ] Load the correct `.env` file (automatically happens if present in root)\n- [ ] Double-check for variable name collisions (e.g. shared `SUPABASE_URL`)\n- [ ] If you use `nvm`, run `nvm use` to match project’s Node version (`.nvmrc`)\n### 💻 Editor & Git Hygiene\n- [ ] Close any leftover tabs from the other project to avoid confusion\n- [ ] Make sure Git commits/branches are being made in the correct repo\n- [ ] Ensure `.gitignore` is respected and not showing temp files from old builds\n### ⚙️ Package Manager Consistency\n- [ ] Stick with the same one (`npm`, `yarn`, or `pnpm`) per project\n- [ ] Delete `node_modules` if switching managers between sessions\n### 🧠 System Resources\n- [ ] If the system is laggy, close other project windows or tabs\n- [ ] Watch for port conflicts or EMFILE errors on large projects\n### 🐳 Backend Services (if used)\n- [ ] Shut down any containers from the previous project (`docker-compose down`)\n- [ ] Avoid port conflicts (e.g. Postgres, Redis, Supabase emulator)\n- [ ] Use project-specific volumes/networks if needed\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n[realestate.com.au]({{http://realestate.com.au}}) scraper: [🏘️ realestate.com.au properties pages scraper ❤️ · Apify]({{https://apify.com/azzouzana/realestate-com-au-properties-pages-scraper}})\n<empty-block/>\nBulk upload (search) instantly \nAI search \n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nI specialise in building web and native applications, integrating AI-driven automations, and SaaS Marketing Strategies. That's it, nothing else.\nWhether it’s crafting a robust web app, streamlining workflows with AI-powered automations, or implementing data-driven SaaS marketing strategies, I deliver solutions that drive measurable impact.\nI don’t just build software—I architect systems designed for growth, efficiency, and seamless user experiences. From custom app development to AI-enhanced process automation, I ensure businesses leverage technology to increase efficiency, reduce costs, and accelerate user acquisition.\nIf you're looking for a technical expert who understands both engineering and growth, let’s connect. I bring a strategic, results-oriented approach to every project, ensuring your software and marketing efforts work together to scale your business. Let’s build something exceptional.\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nTelegram bot:\nbot name: N8N Lead Bot \nbot username: harrysodyssey_bot\nhttp API key: 7935179210:AAFBTxXYnA5DnFiO2X9v3OeAhHJJeoDgWGo\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nIf you are building a vercel serverless function to deploy an API, make sure that in production the package.json file does not have the line “ type:module”.<br>-  this will force vercel to read the file as an ESM module instead of commonJS\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nThings I want to know when I site down to use cursor: \n<empty-block/>\n1. Does the model have context over my code? (code indexed?)\n2. Is MCP being called natively? \n3. Is the build clean and current? \n4. Is the project running locally? \n5. Is my local repo aligned with git? \n6. Am I in an existing git branch? \n7. Are my cursor rules being applied?\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nemployable experiences:\n<empty-block/>\n1. Contentious founder moment (store credit versus instant discounts)\n2. Feature request from early customer (Schmuel - problem: duplicate sales, API solution)\n3. Pivots based on customer needs(Auction platform to Escrow Service to Marketplace and Inventory management)\n<empty-block/>\nCompetent feature build \n<empty-block/>\n1. AI search function in Marketplace \n2. AI image analysis - parameters to retrieve product description and product price \n3. N8N lead generator AND reddit scraper\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<br>Freelancer.com Australian product manager role \n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\npractice questions for interviews:\n<empty-block/>\n1. **We’d love to hear what excites you about this role! How do you see it fitting into your career goals for the next 5 years?**\n\tCompany culture, horizontal workplace, remote, product autonomy, \n<empty-block/>\n1. **What’s an achievement you’re really proud of in product management? Why does it stand out as a meaningful moment for you?**\n\tBuilding something meaning for customers, I’ll be forever chasing that feeling. Discovering a real painpoint and the building a feature to solve it. Dealers were suffering from double selling, so I built an API from their website to inventory so they could update the team on the inventory status. Double selling was significantly reduced.\n<empty-block/>\n1. **Explain the coolest thing you have built with no-code tools in detail from idea to execution**\n\tNatural language search function\n<empty-block/>\n1. **Have you ever done something outside of formal education like taking a course, reading a book, or working on a project to grow professionally? What inspired you to dive into it?**\n\tSelf taught bubble course - going from a non-technical founder to learning to build products by necessity\n<empty-block/>\n1. **Can you share a time when you had to juggle competing priorities? How did you manage it all, and what did you take away from the experience?**\n\tinvestor expectations and building/learning to build the product\n<empty-block/>\n1. **Tell us about a time you received constructive feedback. How did it feel at first, and how did you use it to grow or improve?**\n\tMike, wanting to work on the product alone\n\t<empty-block/>\n\t<empty-block/>\n\tRobot Run Wayne Wang\n\t<empty-block/>\n\t<empty-block/>\n\t<page url=\"{{https://www.notion.so/24d348f4363480a29e08f8eb65ef821f}}\">Project Structure:</page>\n\t<empty-block/>\n\t<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nAI tinkerers:\n<empty-block/>\n- open spec workflow\n- share space \n- Blackbird giant \n<empty-block/>\n---\n<empty-block/>\nDistribution is the real moat \n<empty-block/>\n---\n<empty-block/>\nSAFE raising rhetoric:<br><br>\n<table header-row=\"true\">\n<colgroup>\n<col width=\"382.00001525878906\">\n<col width=\"414.00001525878906\">\n</colgroup>\n<tr>\n<td>**Investor Question**</td>\n<td>**Founder Answer (What You Say)**</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Why are you raising on a SAFE instead of equity?**</td>\n<td>“At this stage, setting a precise valuation is premature and slows things down. A SAFE lets us move quickly, stay focused on building, and reward you with a cap/discount for backing us early.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**What’s the valuation cap, and why that number?**</td>\n<td>“We set the cap based on where we see comparable companies at our stage and the milestones we’ll hit before a Series A. It gives you upside protection if we grow faster than expected.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**How much SAFE capital are you planning to raise?**</td>\n<td>“We’re targeting \\$X in total SAFEs before converting. We’re being deliberate about the size so there’s no messy overhang when we raise the next priced round.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**What happens if you raise more SAFEs after mine? Won’t I get diluted?**</td>\n<td>“All SAFEs convert at the same valuation cap or better, so you won’t be disadvantaged. We’re transparent about the total SAFE pool, and our goal is to keep it clean and limited.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**What ownership will I actually end up with?**</td>\n<td>“It depends on the Series A valuation, but your SAFE cap ensures you’ll convert into equity at no worse than \\$X valuation, which is typically founder-friendly while still fair to early investors.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Why should I invest now instead of waiting for the priced round?**</td>\n<td>“Because your SAFE gives you either a discount or the cap — meaning you’re rewarded for conviction today. If you wait, you’ll invest at a higher valuation without those protections.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Do I get pro rata rights?**</td>\n<td>“SAFEs don’t include them by default, but we can provide a side letter so you’ll have the ability to participate in future rounds.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Aren’t SAFEs risky for investors?**</td>\n<td>“They’re standardised and YC-backed. The terms are simple and clean, and your downside is no different from equity at this stage — you’re betting on the team and traction. The upside is, you’re locked in at a favourable cap.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**What’s stopping you from stacking tons of SAFEs?**</td>\n<td>“Nothing in the legal docs, which is why we’re being upfront: we’re only raising \\$X on SAFEs before converting to equity. That way everyone has clarity and confidence in ownership.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Why not just do a priced round now?**</td>\n<td>“A priced round means higher legal fees, board seats, and heavier dilution before we’ve proven enough. SAFEs let us delay that until Series A, when we’ll have the traction to justify stronger terms for everyone.”</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n<empty-block/>\n---\n<empty-block/>\n## Example: raising 5M SAFE:<br><br>\n### **Scenario Setup**\n- Total raised: **\\$5M**\n- Number of investors: **10**\n- Check size each: **\\$500k**\n- All SAFEs on the **same terms** (same cap, same discount, etc.)\n---\n### **What That Means for Investors**\n- **Conversion mechanics**: Each investor’s \\$500k SAFE will convert into equity at the Series A once you do a priced round.\n- If you set, say, a **\\$20M valuation cap**, each \\$500k would buy:20M500k=2.5% of the company (pre-Series A)\n\t500k20M=2.5% of the company (pre-Series A)\\\\frac\\{500k\\}\\{20M\\} = 2.5\\\\% \\\\text\\{ of the company (pre-Series A)\\}\n- With 10 investors, that’s **25% of the company** allocated across the SAFE pool once they convert.\n---\n### **The SAFE Pool (“Overhang”)**\n- Until the priced round, these 10 investors don’t technically own equity — they’re just owed future equity.\n- When you raise a Series A, all 10 SAFEs convert at the agreed cap (or discount).\n- This creates a **large dilution event** at Series A, since the equity you’ve promised gets crystallised all at once.\n---\n### **How Founders Frame This**\n- **Clarity**: “We’ve capped the SAFE pool at \\$5M, so you know your eventual % ownership isn’t being diluted by endless SAFEs.”\n- **Fairness**: “All investors are on the same terms — no side deals, no last-minute changes.”\n- **Future fundraising**: “This conversion will set us up cleanly for a Series A without overhang confusion.”\n---\n### **Key Considerations**\n1. **Cap Level Matters** — At a \\$20M cap, the SAFE pool is 25%. At a \\$40M cap, it’s \\~12.5%.\n2. **Stacking Risk** — If you raise another SAFE later, all investors share more dilution; investors will ask if \\$5M is the *final* SAFE amount.\n3. **Series A Optics** — Some VCs dislike walking into a round where 25%+ of the company is already allocated to SAFEs (they call it “too much overhang”).\n4. **Pro Rata** — If you gave side letters, each of the 10 investors may also expect to participate in the Series A.\n---\n👉 So in your example, if you raised **\\$5M on SAFEs with a \\$20M cap**, you’ve effectively sold **\\~25% of the company already**, spread evenly across 10 investors. They don’t “own” it yet, but the Series A will crystalise that ownership.\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n---\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nSitemate video Script:\n<empty-block/>\n**Hey everyone at Sitemate - My name is Harry - For my app I’ve designed a high fidelity mockup for a natural language search function that lets that lets workers type plain English to retrieve project information and instantly track tasks.**\nFor workers with deadlines, every second counts so this design aims to:\n- **Cut time-to-first-result** from \\~20–30s of tapping through filters to **\\<5s**.\n- **Lift search success rate** (queries that return ≥1 relevant result) to **\\>90%**.\n- **Reduce “no results” dead-end** via smarter parsing and clear.\nHere you’re seeing and LLM-style composer that people are getting used to now. After submission you can see a small summary of what was applied and  then editable chips base on their query if they want to filter manually, and then results below. It would also offline and have a safe fallback to keyword search, so workers workflows aren’t impeded.\n<empty-block/>\nTo ensure a timely delivery I’d break up the functionality in three safe increments:\n- **The first release would** deliver the regular keyword search, core chips, results, loading/empty/offline, and Save view—our reliable baseline (target **\\<5s** time-to-first-result, **\\>80%** search success).\n- **In the second release we would** add a rule-based natural-language parser (no LLM)\n- **And for the third release we’d** add a small, capped LLM back-stop (rate-limited, cached) only when the rule-based parser is unsure—handling messy phrases.\nI’d collaborate with my engineer initially with an 1 on 1 brief outlining goals, scope, success metrics. I’d want to run  a mid-week review with them and a end of week demo with real queries, but I’d really want to prioritise setting expectations early to give my engineer proper autonomy throughout the week to develop. For a smooth implementation ideally We’d ship behind a the feature to a pilot group, compare against baseline metrics, then roll out broadly once we seeing  faster search results\n<empty-block/>\nIf this aligns with you guys at Sitemate, I’d love to hear back from you. <br><br>Thanks\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nI\n<empty-block/>\nI’d collaborate with my engineer initally with a 1-page brief goals, scope, metrics and expectations on minimum inputs and outputs for returning JSON. \n<empty-block/>\nI’d break up the functionality it in three safe increments: \n- **The first release would** deliver the regular keyword search, core chips, results, loading/empty/offline, and Save view—our reliable baseline (target **\\<5s** time-to-first-result, **\\>80%** search success). \n- **In the first release we would **add initially add a rule-based parser using natural-language that doesn’t use an LLM which should deliver a high success rate **\\>90%** success. \n- **and for the third release we’d** add a small, capped LLM back-stop only when the rule based parser is unsure—handling messy phrases without latency/cost spikes\n<empty-block/>\nWe”d run a mid-week review, and a Friday demo with real queries. I’d be prioritising being intentional about how I set expectations early no so I can give my engineer proper autonomy to implement the feature. \n<empty-block/>\n<br><br>\n---\n<empty-block/>\nLuminance questions \n- does “live projects” mean feature building? \n- How many days in the office each week?\n- Renumeration?\n- Are you guys focusing on Australian expansion now?\n<empty-block/>\n---\nLuminance points:\n- Luminance has context over the organisations legal positions that automatically adjust the word document\n- The repository is the database -  because I’m non-technical and a founder, I’ll be able to communicate with customers in a way that isn’t complex\n- I feel like my study of law in the past will help me relate more when I’m speaking to clients \n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nRequirements for an AI Workflow Engineer (job description)<br><br>**What You Bring (no superhero cape required)**\n- Some **Python** under your belt, plus comfort with AI/automation libraries and APIs\n- Familiarity with workflow tools (Power Automate, Zapier, n8n, Airtable — whatever gets you results)\n- Confidence jumping into cloud basics (AWS, Azure or GCP)\n- A builder’s streak: you like to see things made, working, and delivering value.\n- Experience with TensorFlow, PyTorch, or Hugging Face is a plus (but not essential)\n- Bonus points if you’ve ever proudly said “I built that.”\n- Finally, values that resonate with ours ... you can check them out on the \"About Us\" page at FirstFocus dot com dot au.\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n---\n<empty-block/>\nLearn TensorFlow, PyTorch and HuggingFace\n[PyTorch in 100 Seconds]({{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ORMx45xqWkA}})\n<empty-block/>\n---\n<empty-block/>\nhair stack:\n- Oral Finasteride 1mg or 1ml 0.25% topical 1x per day\n- Oral Minoxidil 5% or topical 2x per day \n- Ketaconazole shampoo 2%\n- Rosemary or Rosehip oil \n- Biotin capsules 1x per day\n- Minox on the beard \n- derma pen \n- Copper peptide ( GHK CU)\n- Fari oil \n- bodywise roll on [Be Bodywise Rosemary Hair Growth Serum (Roll On) \\| 3% Redensyl, 2% Aminexil & 2% Anagain \\| Thicker & Stronger Hair \\| Prevents Hair Fall & Stimulates Hair Follicles \\| For All Hair Types \\| 25ml]({{https://www.amazon.com.au/Be-Bodywise-Rosemary-Stimulates-Follicles/dp/B0CPJB7WW7}})\n- Rosemary Oil with [Best Microneedle Pen – Korabeauticals® V2 Pro]({{https://korabeauticals.com/products/korabeauticals-v2?srsltid=AfmBOooAIUJCTBwOsXFPDBY0l9TjY2nh2x_s-ZVuXQPe7I1_6GH-UFMh}})\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nvitamin stack \n- creatine\n- ashwagahnga \n- tongkat Ali \n<empty-block/>\ntransition to DUT after about a year potentially\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n“””\nJob description I like \n<empty-block/>\n## **About the job**\nThis job is sourced from a job board. **Learn More**\nJob Details\n**About The Role**We’re building a small, agile **Digital Hub** to pioneer practical digital and AI solutions across Borg. To do that, we’re looking for an **AI Solutions Specialist** who can turn AI advancements into tools people actually use.This role is hands-on: prototyping chatbots, building smart assistants, testing predictive models, and embedding AI into workflows. You’ll be working closely with business teams to spot opportunities, prototype quickly, and refine solutions that make work simpler, faster, and smarter.**What You’ll Do**\n- Prototype and deploy AI-powered tools like chatbots, assistants, and search/find features\n- Apply AI APIs (OpenAI, Hugging Face, Azure OpenAI, etc.) to enhance workflows and decision-making\n- Partner with Sales, Marketing, Customer Service, and Logistics to test and refine solutions\n- Analyse datasets to uncover insights and support forecasting and optimisation pilots\n- Embed AI into existing systems (ERP, CRM, and other systems) through APIs and integrations\n- Share knowledge and help drive adoption through demos, training, and user support\n**What You’ll Bring**\n- Strong Python skills for prototyping and data handling\n- Experience with AI/ML libraries (OpenAI, Hugging Face, LangChain, etc.)\n- Ability to analyse and communicate insights with tools like Pandas or Power BI\n- API integration skills (REST, JSON, authentication methods)\n- 2+ years’ experience delivering applied AI or automation projects\n- Strong communicator who can explain AI simply to non-technical users\n**Bonus points for**\n- Knowledge of vector databases (Pinecone, Weaviate, Qdrant)\n- Exposure to business process automation or RPA is a plus\n- Exposure to deep learning frameworks (TensorFlow, PyTorch)\n- Familiarity with Azure, AWS, or GCP”””<br><br><br>job description\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nClass Structure for Learnrooms: \n<empty-block/>\nHow to build a Marketplace with AI: \n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n1. App planning and prompt structure \n2. UX Pilot Wireframes \n3. Authentication\n4. backend integration \n5. Editing you app using prompts \n6. Deployment using Vercel - domain \n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n---\n<empty-block/>\nLuxury Escapes submission checklist \n<empty-block/>\n- [ ] **Cover Slide** – Title, subtitle, your name.\n- [x] **Customer Context** – Who LE customers are; insight on different intent types; main pain points (discovery + decision).\n- [x] **Booking Journey Map** – 5 stages; highlight focus on Discovery + Decision.\n- [x] **Problems to Solve** – Discovery = rigid filters for fuzzy intent; Decision = unclear availability/flexibility.\n- [x] **Feature 1: Lux (AI Travel Companion)** – Explain what it is, how it works, example queries, benefits.\n- [ ] **Feature 2: Smart Availability Indicator** – Show what it displays (dates, cancellation, nudges), benefits.\n- [x] **Prioritisation** – RICE or 2x2 grid; Availability = quick win, Lux = strategic differentiator.\n- [x] **User Stories** – One for Lux (discovery), one for Availability (decision).\n- [x] **Success Metrics / KPIs** – Conversion, drop-off, NPS, repeat bookings; mapped to each feature.\n- [ ] **Wrap-Up** – Reiterate LE’s uniqueness (curated luxury + flexibility) and how these features strengthen it.\n- [ ] **Visuals** – Add at least 1 mockup for Lux search + 1 for availability calendar.\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<table header-row=\"true\">\n<tr>\n<td>Category</td>\n<td>Lux Feature / Capability</td>\n<td>Rationale</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Must Have**</td>\n<td>Text-based natural language search (plain English input).</td>\n<td>Core functionality that removes friction in discovery; enables customers to describe trips conversationally.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Must Have**</td>\n<td>Hybrid search (structured filters + semantic intent).</td>\n<td>Ensures queries like “romantic Bali trip under \\$6k” map correctly to destinations, budget, and inclusions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Should Have**</td>\n<td>Contextual suggestions if no perfect match is found.</td>\n<td>Prevents dead ends (“no results”), keeps customers engaged by offering close alternatives.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Should Have**</td>\n<td>Simple integration into existing search UI.</td>\n<td>Maintains continuity for users who prefer traditional filters while enabling Lux as an added option.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Could Have**</td>\n<td>Voice input for mobile app.</td>\n<td>Enhances premium feel and convenience, but not critical for first release.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Could Have**</td>\n<td>Personalised recommendations based on past searches.</td>\n<td>Adds value over time but requires more customer data and trust safeguards.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Won’t Have (for now)**</td>\n<td>Proactive, concierge-style trip planning (Lux suggesting trips unprompted).</td>\n<td>Ambitious and high value long term, but outside scope of improving the booking process immediately.</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nsitemate UX PIlot prompts:\n<empty-block/>\n1. page context:\n“””Design a SINGLE high-fidelity MOBILE screen for a Sitemate app feature: **Tasks — Search & Filter**. The purpose is to help workers run projects and track tasks quickly in the field.\nPlatform & frame\n- iOS light theme, iPhone 15 frame (390×844). Use 8-pt spacing. Minimum tap target 44px.\nLayout (top → bottom)\n1. App bar with title “Tasks”.\n2. Sticky Search bar (placeholder: “Search tasks, people, sites…”). Trailing icons: mic, filter. Include a subtle loading spinner state when typing.\n3. (Optional NL hint) Small line under search that appears after submit:<br>“Interpreted: Status ≠ Done • Project: Barangaroo • Assignee: Jess • Due: Overdue”<br>Each interpreted item should look like a small chip.\n4. Quick filter chips row (toggles): Assigned to me, Overdue, Today, Open, Has photos. Include a “Clear” text button at end.\n5. Results list of compact **Task cards** (6–8 items). Each card shows:\n\t- Title (e.g., “RFI #1428 – Podium slab penetration”)\n\t- Status pill (Open / In progress / Blocked / Done)\n\t- Due (date or “Overdue 3d”)\n\t- Project/Site (e.g., “Barangaroo Tower B”)\n\t- Assignee avatar + short name (e.g., Jess S.)\n\t- Chevron \\> affordance\n6. Sticky footer bar:\n\t- Left: “Showing 24 results”\n\t- Right primary button: “Save view”\nStates (show as overlays/variants within this one screen)\n- Loading: 5–6 skeleton task cards.\n- Empty: friendly illustration + “No matches. Clear filters or try fewer terms.”\n- Offline: top banner “Results from last sync • Try again”.\nVisual system\n- Typeface: Inter or SF Pro (Title 17–18 semibold; metadata 12–13).\n- Colour tokens:\n\t- Primary #1565C0\n\t- Text #0F172A\n\t- Muted #64748B\n\t- Chip outline #E5E7EB, active fill #E3F2FD, active text #0B62B3\n\t- Overdue/Warning #DC2626, Success #16A34A\n\t- Surfaces #FFFFFF\n- Soft elevation/shadow on search and footer to imply stickiness.\n- Icons: search, microphone, filter, chevron-right, calendar.\nMicrocopy examples (use as realistic content)\n- “Defect – Handrail loose level 12 core” • Due Fri • **Blocked** • Barangaroo Tower B • Jess S.\n- “Inspection – Fire collar install, basement 2 zone C” • Due Today • **Open** • Parramatta Site A • Alex L.\n- “RFI #1428 – Podium slab penetration” • Overdue 3d • **In progress** • Barangaroo Tower B • Priya K.\nInteraction notes\n- Tapping chips toggles filled/outlined state; count in footer updates (“Showing N results”).\n- Tapping “Save view” shows a small toast: “Saved as ‘Morning Check’”.\n- Keep everything within ONE frame; use component variants/overlays for states.\nAccessibility\n- AA contrast on text and pills. Clear focus/pressed states on chips and cards.\nGoal\n- Communicate speed, clarity, and reliability of searching & filtering tasks for field teams in a single, production-ready screen.””””\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n1. Screen context\n“””Design a SINGLE high-fidelity MOBILE screen for Sitemate: **Tasks — Smart Search & Filter**. Make the NATURAL-LANGUAGE capability unmistakable, inspired by ChatGPT’s composer. One frame only.\nPlatform & constraints\n- iOS light theme, iPhone 15 (390×844). 8-pt spacing. 44px min touch. Use component variants/overlays for states (no extra screens).\nTop “ChatGPT-style” area\n- Hero title centered: **Where should we begin?**\n- Large pill-shaped **composer** with leading \\[+\\] insert button, placeholder that ROTATES through examples:\n\t1. “overdue inspections at Barangaroo by Jess”\n\t2. “open defects due this week, not done”\n\t3. “RFIs tagged electrical, sort by priority”\n- Trailing icons: mic and ✨ (Smart). Subheader under the field: **“Ask in plain English to filter tasks. Not a chat — built for search.”**\n- Row of **suggestion chips/cards** under the composer (prefill on tap): *Overdue at my sites · Open defects due this week · RFIs by Jess not done · Show mine only*.\nWhile typing\n- Recognised entities tokenise inline as **smart tokens**: `@Jess` (assignee), `#Barangaroo` (project), `status:open`, `due:this week`. Provide lightweight autocomplete on `@`, `#`, `:`.\nOn submit (same screen)\n- A compact **assistant-style summary bubble** appears above results:<br>“I’ve applied: Status ≠ Done • Due: Overdue • Project: Barangaroo • Assignee: Jess.”<br>Links: **Edit filters** · **View JSON** (tiny overlay card showing a short `{filters:{…}}` snippet).\n- Group parsed chips under **From your query ✨** (filled blue with sparkle icon). Each chip has a tiny edit pencil.\n- Below, a second row **More filters** (outlined grey chips): Assigned to me, Today, Open, Has photos.\nResults list (core)\n- 6–8 compact **Task cards** with: Title; Status pill (Open/In progress/Blocked/Done); Due (e.g., “Overdue 3d”, “Due Today”); Project/Site; Assignee avatar+short name; chevron ›.\n- Sample content:\n\t- “RFI #1428 – Podium slab penetration” • **In progress** • Overdue 3d • Barangaroo Tower B • Priya K.\n\t- “Defect – Handrail loose level 12 core” • **Blocked** • Due Fri • Barangaroo Tower B • Jess S.\n\t- “Inspection – Fire collar, basement 2 zone C” • **Open** • Due Today • Parramatta Site A • Alex L.\nFooter\n- Sticky bar: left **“Showing 24 results”**; right primary button **“Save as Smart view”** (toast: “Saved as ‘Morning Check’”).\nStates (overlays/variants within this frame)\n- **Thinking/Loading:** assistant bubble shows typing dots for a moment + skeleton task cards.\n- **Empty:** friendly illustration + “No matches. Try: ‘open defects due Friday’ or ‘my RFIs at Site A’.”\n- **Offline:** top banner “Showing cached results • Try again”.\nVisual system\n- Typeface: Inter/SF Pro (title 18 semibold; body 16; meta 12–13).\n- Colours: Primary #1565C0; Text #0F172A; Muted #64748B; Surface #FFFFFF; Chip outline #E5E7EB; Active chip fill #E3F2FD + text #0B62B3; Warning/Overdue #DC2626; Success #16A34A.\n- Soft shadows on composer and footer to imply stickiness. Clean, spacious top area like ChatGPT home.\nGoal\n- Immediately signal “type full sentences” via ChatGPT-like cues; show explainability (summary bubble, tokens, ✨ chips); and retain deterministic filters and results — all on ONE production-ready screen.”””\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n### **Organisational and Implementation Considerations**\n- **Developer Communication**:\n\tThese features are modular and can be layered on top of the existing booking infrastructure. Clear communication with developers will be essential around:\n\t- How package metadata is structured and indexed.\n\t- Where Lux plugs into the search experience without disrupting existing filters.\n\t- How availability and flexibility data is sourced and displayed consistently across packages.\n\t\tEarly collaboration with engineering will reduce risk of scope creep and ensure both features integrate seamlessly into the current stack.\n- **Stakeholder Risks**:\n\t- **Marketing/Brand**: Lux must feel like a *concierge*, not a chatbot. Design and tone will need close alignment with the brand team to maintain LE’s premium positioning.\n\t- **Operations**: Availability and flexibility rules often depend on third-party suppliers (hotels, tour operators). Ensuring data accuracy and clarity will require coordination with supplier management teams.\n**Customer Support**\n: Both features could increase inbound questions initially. Support teams should be briefed and involved in testing to anticipate customer reactions.\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n## **Lux Companion MoSCoW Feature Prioritisation:**\n<table>\n<tr>\n<td>**Category**</td>\n<td>**Lux Feature / Capability**</td>\n<td>**Rationale**</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Must Have**</td>\n<td>Text-based natural language search (plain English input)</td>\n<td>Core functionality that removes friction in discovery; enables customers to describe trips conversationally.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Must Have**</td>\n<td>Hybrid search (structured filters + semantic intent)</td>\n<td>Ensures queries like “romantic Bali trip under \\$6k” map correctly to destinations, budget, and inclusions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Should Have**</td>\n<td>Contextual suggestions if no perfect match is found</td>\n<td>Prevents dead ends (“no results”), keeps customers engaged by offering close alternatives.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Should Have**</td>\n<td>Simple integration into existing search UI</td>\n<td>Maintains continuity for users who prefer traditional filters while enabling Lux as an added option.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Could Have**</td>\n<td>Voice input for mobile app</td>\n<td>Enhances premium feel and convenience, but not critical for first release.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Could Have**</td>\n<td>Personalised recommendations based on past searches</td>\n<td>Adds value over time but requires more customer data and trust safeguards.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Won’t Have**</td>\n<td>Proactive concierge-style trip planning (unprompted suggestions)</td>\n<td>Ambitious and high-value long term, but outside scope of improving the booking process immediately.</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nTwo ideal cofounders: \n- Inventor \n- Entrepreneur \n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nAI Marketing \n- N8N workflow brainrot \n- For Disco AI UGC\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n---\n<empty-block/>\nI decided to endeavour in the riskiest form of business and I didn’t even know it: Inventing and innovation. \n<empty-block/>\n---\n<empty-block/>\npreferred tech stack for a role:<br><br>”””\nIf you focus on insights/analysis, our preferred tech is python/pandas/jupyter notebooks. If you’re doing an interactive interface, we prefer python/flask/vanilla JS.\nDefinitely leave time to describe your work. Candidates that only submit code will not have their code reviewed. You are free to use LLMs as much as you like.””\n<empty-block/>\n---\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nMy business pursuits: \n1. Disco \n2. Bidet \n3. Meditatetions\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n---\n<empty-block/>\nGoogle Auth Credentials for N8N Google Sheets<br><br>Client ID: [757825509547-hqmr92pjk8k6mck37cmq87ishofugp1o.apps.googleusercontent.com]({{http://757825509547-hqmr92pjk8k6mck37cmq87ishofugp1o.apps.googleusercontent.com/}})\n<empty-block/>\nClient Secret: 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\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nOpenAI Secret Key: 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\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nGoogle Auth Credentials for N8N Google Drive<br><br>Client ID: [757825509547-32ngse2icvsb7lq7ph1ogul55ejdd137.apps.googleusercontent.com]({{http://757825509547-32ngse2icvsb7lq7ph1ogul55ejdd137.apps.googleusercontent.com/}})\n<empty-block/>\nClient Secret: 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\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n---\n<empty-block/>\nAI Image generation stack:\n- Higgsfield \n- Kie API \n- Vertex AI \n- Airtable\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nHera: motion Video AI [Hera – Your AI Motion Designer]({{https://hera.video/}})\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nHey team at Zimozi!<br>\nSuper excited about this role - I’m particularly interested in its intersection of using technical capabilities to better deliver customer support. <br><br>I’ve built a couple of full stack products with PostgreSQL database integration (Supabase) and have executed serverless edge functions to improve user experiences. \n<empty-block/>\nI’ve also built purposeful features for early users by conducting interviews to properly understand their pain points, so I feel this would translate well into a technical customer support role. \n<empty-block/>\nOh, and I love building automations and dabbling with AI agents in N8N! I’ve got a couple of workflows in my CV you can read about. \n<empty-block/>\nLooking forward to hearing from you, <br><br>Harry\n<empty-block/>\n[https://youtu.be/oAWe5wFwHlo?si=KVELKTwnlcR_XCV-]({{https://youtu.be/oAWe5wFwHlo?si=KVELKTwnlcR_XCV-}})\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\nScreen studio:\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n---\n<empty-block/>\n[IREN \\| Next-Gen Data Centers for AI, HPC & Sustainable Compute]({{https://iren.com/}})\n<empty-block/>\nData centre company in the US \nAustralian founders \n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n---\nSQL legacy on .net \nheavy load on database \ncustomer keep emailing support role \n- datalake - t\n- train onsite customer engagement team on data dashbaord and visualisation \n<empty-block/>\noffshore CTO and a singapore guy \n<empty-block/>\n- any equipment that i need for the job?\n- my SQL experience is largely executing phrases in Supabase to create functionality in the apps I’ve built -\n\t- edge functions for Server side functionality for a gambling \n- Inventory management system for watch dealers \n- Workflow that creates a leadlist for a marketing agency who wanted to intercept companies hiring for sales devlopment representatives \n- Workflow that automates AI video generation (Idea, prompt and Video) - final video put into a google sheet \n- I’m happy to commit to 1 year, it aligns well - I’m working on a couple of side projects, but they will a lower priority\n- I’m familiar with postgreSQL databases like supabase, table relationships, foreign key constraints, row level security \n- My techstack: React + TypeScript PWA built with Vite, styled with Tailwind CSS and Radix UI, using Supabase for backend/data, and supporting Ethereum (MetaMask/Ethers.js) and Solana wallets for crypto transactions \n- “Knowledgebase support articles” - having studied law i can interpret complex scenarios into plain and easy to understand structures which will help across the team - so I’ll easily be able to create jnowledge bases\n- Zendesk - ticketing system\n\t- Zendesk includes a **Help Centre** — a collection of “how-to” articles, guides, and FAQs.\n\t- As the job description mentions, you’ll **create Knowledge Base articles** so customers (and internal staff) can solve issues faster.\n\t- It\n<empty-block/>\n**Tasks & responsibilities**\n- You will provide Level 1 Helpdesk Customer Support via Telephone, Zendesk and Email.\n- You will document all Help Desk correspondence through internal systems (Zendesk) and communicate effectively, calmly and in a timely manner with customers during and after resolution of identified support requirements.\n- You will create Knowledge Base Support articles for internal and external stakeholders\n<empty-block/>\nQuestions\n- Dohra said that there might be potentially opportunities to jump in and learn new skills around development and workflow automation - could you guys tell me a little bit more?\n- Tool subscroptions like Cursor and SaaS product be covered by the company?\n- Would there potentially be a possibility of extending my role into development? \n- What’s the volume of tickets like and whats the persona \n- As a level 1, what expectation will there be for me resolving the issue, and when will it be expected for me to escalate to level 2?\n- Over christmas, what will be the expectations?\n<empty-block/>\n---\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n![](https://prod-files-secure.s3.us-west-2.amazonaws.com/f517d51c-d559-4a6e-a307-29e438b5b332/2f5d6cc7-962f-4ca4-9cfa-a3dd8b16940d/Screenshot_2025-11-15_at_12.51.10_pm.png?X-Amz-Algorithm=AWS4-HMAC-SHA256&X-Amz-Content-Sha256=UNSIGNED-PAYLOAD&X-Amz-Credential=ASIAZI2LB4666F2FQNX3%2F20260223%2Fus-west-2%2Fs3%2Faws4_request&X-Amz-Date=20260223T085031Z&X-Amz-Expires=3600&X-Amz-Security-Token=IQoJb3JpZ2luX2VjEBEaCXVzLXdlc3QtMiJHMEUCIQDmrL7OdVI5NkcULlyjWeeyjikuyVOda2YTuwSRy5IMoQIgWS7qpc%2FWhB%2BFh%2BSQzazQUkJLOogXyBqmE4%2Fc0%2FdPhs0qiAQI2v%2F%2F%2F%2F%2F%2F%2F%2F%2F%2FARAAGgw2Mzc0MjMxODM4MDUiDEwu1G6vdJul9XFo6SrcA%2FXQnslwFwZevobODrtY7h%2B0CoLXOAxyLQSB%2FPtcTjfwBl%2B%2BFPQOc1VcY9uHAE6u%2FFEB65eebo9j1cL9k1DPW9cyGVut%2Bd4qYoyu2GNoIeVi57U9uFrUcyObhgWmsFBy%2Fnvqq0uTRyYg0fObAHsk4NoMjXcVPfyhCtjREWWxnkNOdr51Om%2FTHY0czNRNbNZOU3DWsg6tSd04poGdQiLiGVQyqpSBfJqjiZHNGO0Wvkq27LBP22LVu19c1TfVhUERpqU2wRv%2F47B2guMEK3xYbcfO67POCQPiwD4bP7T90YVYwk9xbIsWc5A%2F%2BB5uOzb5uxcFoEReFC2438QBjrMuRXMFTKzwz1c6CaqLHv3zSS2%2B8dgI13io1dnOdnC2LPDblZLpe78J5VWG4hDw3t7eFEqlAcQmqGNdyyHpUGtvIv7bV4EFjZ0ERA3wDOvS9zpKeYacPPTaBiTE8Uv4dhlLIyDTnUCS5zUligh808J4UNR%2Bpg%2F6LqXfDkAK2lK5CGDrXUi2OZz5j5O1%2F0Ae7PvGHOsytFnnw8tkUl4ojOTn%2BUti8npBo5f%2F5aj8Zu17%2BEyPJCoayh2Owa4jR7bZlz6rA9RGrbABj8PASWXkfyfFlXjOpLZrYL3k1BPWj3i7MJil8MwGOqUBJ%2F9%2BqJH9oTN0SMYCLdcCXbP70PNK6AvSpfPP7KIfxp%2BXhBEzi28SbYQ4ltcJUZH8qSGJP%2FSkn9TeNPHtptSsofii%2B2x1NcaJHBUniokErJ4YGMh5cZpdcJQN%2FFp6sUR7ebwwQ%2BFOnreyKqHBlW650qjmK81WiqrtL0P2dNlOwm25qfD3gK7Ui%2FVOll0WZQ8ENcy0BZsTum%2B5jpCR%2F3K75HTUmOYC&X-Amz-Signature=8368e497ffccf78f6e9ba263aadc704378202bbe6ada1a1b8b5e641c0913575b&X-Amz-SignedHeaders=host&x-amz-checksum-mode=ENABLED&x-id=GetObject)\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n---\n<empty-block/>\nMy number is \\$100M\n<empty-block/>\n---\n<empty-block/>\nNice sites:\n<empty-block/>\n1. [The Leaders in Account-to-Account Payment Solutions]({{https://zepto.com.au/}})\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n---\n<empty-block/>\nproduct analytics:\nevents: mixpanel, amplitude, heap\ndashboards/reporting: [quill.co]({{https://quill.co/}}), metabase, grafana, redash\ndata/data stack: dbt, mozart data\nstill undefeated -- talk to your users :)\n<empty-block/>\n---\n<empty-block/>\nDividends and profit splits in a company:\n<empty-block/>\n“””In a high-growth startup, **profits are rarely \"split\" in the early years.** Instead, they are reinvested into the company to fuel user acquisition and scale. However, because you are building a sweepstakes business—which can be \"cash-flow heavy\"—you need a plan for when the company actually has excess cash.<br>In the 51/49 or 55/45 dynamic we discussed, here is how the money actually moves:**<br>1. The Hierarchy of Payments**<br>Before anyone \"splits\" a profit, the company must meet its obligations. In a professional startup, the order of cash flow looks like this:<br>1. **Operating Expenses (OpEx):** AWS/Supabase bills, OpenAI API costs, legal retainers, and prize fulfillment.<br>2. **Market-Rate Salaries:** Once the company is making money, both you and the CMO should draw a salary. This is better for taxes and prevents one person from feeling like they are \"funding\" the other's life.<br>3. **The \"War Chest\" (Retained Earnings):** You keep 3–6 months of runway in the bank for marketing spikes or legal emergencies.<br>4. **Distributions (The \"Profit Split\"):** Only after the above are met do you consider taking money out.**<br>2. How the Split Works (Dividends)**<br>If you decide to take a profit distribution (called a **Dividend**), the law (and your Operating Agreement) typically dictates that it must be **Pro-Rata**.<br>• **If you own 55% and he owns 45%:** If you decide to distribute €10,000 in profit, you *must* take €5,500 and he *must* take €4,500. You cannot easily change this split without creating a tax nightmare or legal \"breach of fiduciary duty.\"<br>• **The Trap:** If you give him 50% now, you are locked into giving him 50% of every spare Euro the company ever makes, even if you are doing 90% of the work three years from now.**<br>3. Handling the \"Salary vs. Profit\" Tension**<br>This is where most co-founder fights happen.<br>• **The Engineer's View:** \"We should keep the money in the company to build more features.\"<br>• **The CMO's View:** \"I need to spend this money on influencers to get more users.\"<br>The Solution: The Board Vote<br>Even in a 2-person company, you act as the \"Board.\"<br>• Since you have **51%**, you have the final say on the **Budget**.<br>• You can decide that \"The company will not pay dividends this year; all profit will be used for a 51/49 split of the new R&D budget.\"**<br>4. Better Alternatives for a \"Full-Time\" CMO**<br>If the CMO needs cash to live on but you want to keep the equity split uneven, use these tools:<br>• **Preferred Salary:** You can agree that he gets a slightly higher monthly salary in the beginning because he put in the 30k cash, while you keep the higher equity.<br>• **Bonus Structure:** Instead of splitting *all* profits, create a \"Performance Bonus.\"<br>    ◦ *Example:* \"If we hit €50k profit this month, the CMO gets a €2k bonus for hitting his CAC (Customer Acquisition Cost) targets.\" This keeps him hungry without giving away the long-term equity.**<br>Summary Table: 51/49 SplitFeatureYou (51%)Him (49%)Voting PowerTie-breaker.** You decide if profit is reinvested or paid out.Must negotiate/convince you to take money out.**Profit Split**You get €51 for every €100 distributed.He gets €49 for every €100 distributed.**Exit (Sale)**You get 51% of the acquisition price.He gets 49% of the acquisition price.**<br>My Recommendation**<br>Do not agree to a \"Profit Split\" that is different from your \"Equity Split.\" It makes the company \"messy\" for future investors. Instead:<br>1. **Keep the Equity at 51/49** (or whatever you negotiate).<br>2. **Pay yourselves equal salaries** once profitable.<br>3. **Reinvest 90% of profits** back into growth (this is how you reach a multi-million dollar exit).<br>**Would you like me to draft a \"Profit Reinvestment Clause\" you can show him to explain why you aren't planning on taking huge payouts in year one?”””**\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n---\n<empty-block/>\nItem photo background removal\n### **AI background removal models**\n- **U²-Net**\n- **MODNet**\n- **Segment Anything (SAM) + refinement**\n- **RemBG** (Python wrapper around U²-Net)\nExample bulk flow:\n```plain text\n/input-images\n→background removal\n→ transparent PNG\n→resizeto1024×1024\n→ centred withpadding\n→ /output-images\n\n\n```\nThis is how marketplaces and sweepstakes sites prep thousands of items.\n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\ntransparent PNG sites:\n1. CleanPNG \n2. TopPNG \n<empty-block/>\n<empty-block/>\n</content>\n</page>"},{"page_id":"188348f436348041aa29ff0b7fd334f0","title":"Communicating product learnings","teamspace_id":"134849a8-d323-4b8f-8f91-ce22ccf5b337","text":"Here is the result of \"view\" for the Page with URL {{https://www.notion.so/188348f436348041aa29ff0b7fd334f0}} as of 2025-01-27T08:13:25.305Z:\n<page url=\"{{https://www.notion.so/188348f436348041aa29ff0b7fd334f0}}\">\n<ancestor-path>\n<parent-page url=\"{{https://www.notion.so/147348f43634806789d7e4fea9f6de2e}}\" title=\"3. The Right Product (What)\"/>\n<ancestor-2-page url=\"{{https://www.notion.so/2aaa314cafd04716abb4768abdd224e2}}\" title=\"Product Almanac \"/>\n</ancestor-path>\n<properties>\n{\"title\":\"Communicating product learnings\"}\n</properties>\n<content>\n<empty-block/>\nEvery week or two, the head of product should take 15-30 mins to highlight what has been learned in product discovery across the various teams.\nThis is meant to encompass bigger learnings only: what worked, what didn't work, and what the teams will be working on next week.\nIt is even more important to share when things didn't go as planned\nBe generous in sharing what you learn and what has worked/not worked.\n</content>\n</page>\n"},{"page_id":"5401e6ad108747c789a3b3015a71a888","title":"Agile Development","teamspace_id":"134849a8-d323-4b8f-8f91-ce22ccf5b337","contentMaxLength":7500,"text":""},{"page_id":"cf0fd0b8bc0b4611b8a7ddd34d5ae850","title":"Idea Dump","teamspace_id":"134849a8-d323-4b8f-8f91-ce22ccf5b337","contentMaxLength":7500,"text":""},{"page_id":"9af28eeec2934258a4386cf9d5c7a29f","title":"Being in Product","teamspace_id":"134849a8-d323-4b8f-8f91-ce22ccf5b337","text":""}]